loride channels from <italic>Torpedo</italic> electroplax: activation by protons. <source>J. Gen. Physiol</source>. <volume>82</volume>:<fpage>25</fpage>&#x02013;45.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6310023</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib10"><citation citation-type="journal">Hamill, O.P., A. Marty, E. Neher, B. Sakmann, and F.J. Sigworth. <year>1981</year>. Improved patch-clamp techniques for high-resolution current recording from cells and cell-free membrane patches. <source>Pflugers Arch.</source> <volume>391</volume>:<fpage>85</fpage>&#x02013;100.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6270629</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal">Jentsch, T.J., T. Friedrich, A. Schriever, and H. Yamada. <year>1999</year>. The CLC chloride channel family. <source>Pfl&#x000fc;gers Arch.-Eur. J. Physiol.</source> <volume>437</volume>:<fpage>783</fpage>&#x02013;795.</citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">Jentsch, T.J., V. Stein, F. Weinreich, and A. Zdebik. <year>2002</year>. Molecular structure and physiological function of chloride channels. <source>Physiol. Rev.</source> <volume>82</volume>:<fpage>503</fpage>&#x02013;568.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11917096</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Jentsch, T.J., K. Steinmeyer, and G. Schwarz. <year>1990</year>. Primary structure of <italic>Torpedo marmorata</italic> chloride channel isolated by expression cloning in <italic>Xenopus</italic> oocytes. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>348</volume>:<fpage>510</fpage>&#x02013;514.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2174129</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Lin, C.-W., and T.-Y. Chen. <year>2000</year>. Cysteine modification of a putative pore residue in ClC-0: implication for the pore stoichiometry of ClC chloride channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>116</volume>:<fpage>535</fpage>&#x02013;546.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11004203</pub-id></citation></ref><re